   "evidence": "HEAD accepts an unmarked orphan when `parents.get(pid) === 1`, its birth time is after invocation start, and `path === directory || path.startsWith(...)`. No ancestry or invocation marker is required. The reproduction launched the empty-environment, detached sleep through an independent launcher. HEAD killed it; running the same probe against base preserved it (`unrelatedAlive:true`). Base signals only the invocation's process group."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "While an invocation is running in a worktree, some unrelated process with no visible environment, whose parent has already exited, starts in that worktree or the scratch directory. When the invocation ends, Limitless treats it as its own and kills it.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/adj/c1.ts $PWD  (runProcess runs `sleep 1.5` in a temp dir; meanwhile the outer test process, not the invocation, starts `/bin/sh -c '/bin/sleep 30 &'` with env={}, detached, in the same dir, so the sleep is orphaned)",
    "output": "{\"unrelatedPid\":21811,\"unrelatedAlive\":false}"
   },
   "evidence": "processes-darwin.ts:52-70: `if (parents.get(pid) !== 1) return false; const born = readBorn(); ... if (born < started) return false; const cwd = readCwd(); ... return directories.some((directory) => path === directory || path.startsWith(...))`. Line 189 `if (offset === length) hidden.add(pid);` and line 227 `for (const pid of hidden) if (parents.get(pid) === 1 && isMember(pid)) marked.add(pid);`. Ownership comes only from being an orphan with an empty environment, born after the invocation started, with a cwd under its roots. Nothing ties the process to this invocation. Base had no such fallback; it only killed its own process group."
  }
